================================================================================
## Vendor all runtime dependencies as wheels (offline Apps install)
================================================================================

Context: The abacus workspace has no PyPI egress (Apps builds, serverless and
classic compute all fail to reach pypi.org / files.pythonhosted.org), so every
deploy since November failed at "Package installation" — even with
requirements.txt reduced to `uv`. Following hello-abacus's
scripts/build_artifact.sh, both apps now ship every locked dependency as a
wheel for the Apps runtime (CPython 3.11, Linux x86_64) and install with no
package index.

Changes:

1. scripts/build_artifact.sh (new) + scripts/_internal/_vendor_artifact.py (new)
   Export uv.lock (main: --extra lakebase), resolve for the Apps platform,
   download wheels (sdist-only pins such as promise are built and must be pure
   Python), split wheels over 9 MiB into .partNN chunks for the main app, and
   check every wheel against uv.lock's sha256. Skips when lock.sha256 matches;
   --force rebuilds, --check verifies offline.
2. vendor_bootstrap.py (new) — main app command
   Reassemble chunks, install vendor/requirements.txt with --no-index
   --no-deps into a writable target (uv, falling back to pip), once per lock,
   then exec run.py with the target first on PYTHONPATH.
3. requirements.txt
   Installs nothing at build time (the chunked wheels cannot go through pip).
4. src/mcp-server/requirements.txt
   --no-index --find-links ./vendor/wheels -r vendor/requirements.txt
   (all MCP wheels are under the limit, so the Apps build installs them).
5. app.yaml.template / src/mcp-server/app.yaml(.template)
   Commands no longer use `uv run`: `python vendor_bootstrap.py` and
   `python -c "from server.main import main; main()"`.
6. databricks.yml / .databricksignore / .gitignore
   Force-sync the gitignored vendor/ dirs; exclude hello-abacus/ from the app.
7. Makefile / scripts/deploy.sh
   `make artifact`, `make artifact-check`; deploy.sh builds (or, on --dry-run,
   checks) the artifact before validate and verifies the vendor files synced.
8. .github/workflows/build-artifact.yml (new)
   Builds and checks both vendor dirs on GitHub-hosted runners (manual run,
   or a push/PR touching either uv.lock or the build scripts) and uploads
   them as the ontobricks-vendor-<sha> artifact (30-day retention). Unzip at
   the root of the same commit, then `make artifact-check && make deploy`.
